  "step_2": "The individual reports comparing probabilities and losses. They note that in Lottery B, a loss is very likely, and they reflect on whether risking a higher loss is worth it. They explicitly state that the probability of no loss (i.e., 0 Euro outcome) is over 50% in A (which is incorrect—A has a 10% chance of 0 Euro; this seems to be a mistake in the report or reasoning) and only 10% in B (also incorrect—B has 55% chance of 0 Euro). Despite the factual errors, the justification centers on the higher chance of avoiding a loss in A compared to B. The individual appears to focus on the probability of avoiding any loss (i.e., getting 0 Euro) and prefers the lottery where this chance is perceived as higher. Their justification is based on a comparison of the likelihood of zero outcomes, even though their stated probabilities are reversed. The deliberation emphasizes avoiding loss and the perceived likelihood of escaping a loss, not on reweighting small probabilities or applying a systematic transformation to low-probability events.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on reweighting outcomes with less than 20% probability—specifically, increasing their weight by 10 percentage points—and then evaluating lotteries based on this transformed expectation. Only Lottery A has an outcome (0 Euro) with less than 20% probability (10%), so it would be the only one affected. The individual, however, does not mention any adjustment of probabilities or special attention to low-probability events. Instead, they focus on the overall chance of avoiding a loss and incorrectly state the probabilities. Their reasoning appears to be based on a misperception of the actual probabilities, particularly reversing the chances of zero outcomes. There is no indication that they overweighted the 10% chance in A or applied any formal rule to small probabilities. The justification is more aligned with a simple comparison of avoidance of loss, possibly with a preference for higher (perceived) chances of zero loss, rather than a weighting rule for rare events. The information considered (avoiding loss, perceived likelihood) does not match the structured focus on small probabilities and reweighting. The rule-based transformation central to the decision reason is absent in the verbal report. Therefore, it is unlikely the individual used this specific decision reason.",
